    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle \mathbf {Hilb} (n,P)}</annotation>
  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/3e5a983f96e02fb15e733a46e57b786b5972dd60" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -0.838ex; width:11.044ex; height:2.843ex;" alt="{\displaystyle \mathbf {Hilb} (n,P)}"/></span> is non-empty then it is linearly connected. So two subschemes of projective space are in the same connected component of the Hilbert scheme <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/If_and_only_if" title="If and only if" id="mwhQ">if and only if</a> they have the same Hilbert polynomial.</p>

<p id="mwhg">Hilbert schemes can have bad singularities, such as irreducible components that are non-reduced at all points. They can also have irreducible components of unexpectedly high dimension. For example, one might expect the Hilbert scheme of <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt85" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwhw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"d"}},"i":0}}]}'>d</span> points (more precisely dimension 0, length <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt86" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwiA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"d"}},"i":0}}]}'>d</span> subschemes) of a scheme of dimension <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt87" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwiQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"n"}},"i":0}}]}'>n</span> to have dimension <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt88" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwig" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"dn"}},"i":0}}]}'>dn</span>, but if <span class="texhtml " about="#mwt89" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwiw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"math","href":"./Template:Math"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"&apos;&apos;n&apos;&apos; ≥ 3"}},"i":0}}]}'><i>n</i> ≥ 3</span> its irreducible components can have much larger dimension.</p>

</section></section></section><section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwjA" aria-labelledby="Functorial_interpretation"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Functorial_interpretation">Functorial interpretation</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Hilbert_scheme&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7" title="Edit section: Functorial interpretation"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div><p id="mwjQ">
There is an alternative interpretation of the Hilbert scheme which leads to a generalization of relative Hilbert schemes parameterizing subschemes of a relative scheme. For a fixed base scheme <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt90" id="mwjg" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"S"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle S}">
